<!--
  The two control rules M16 adds, and the reason they are here rather than in a Style Selector on a
  bare element (PLAN.md §11 M16).

  The High Contrast overlay this milestone deleted was built from bare element selectors —
  Style Selector="Button", Style Selector="Border" — which survived only because it set colours and
  nothing else. The moment a Padding or a MinHeight joined them, the ComboBox drop-down toggle and
  the ScrollBar repeat buttons would have deformed with them, because those are Buttons too.
  Structural rules therefore go in a ControlTheme, which applies to the controls that ASK for it.

  PRIMARY BUTTON. A Setter on Background alone would not have survived a mouse: Fluent's own
  :pointerover and :pressed rules target the template's ContentPresenter, so the accent would vanish
  the instant the pointer touched it and come back when it left. The states are therefore restated
  here, each keeping the accent and each carrying its measured foreground.
-->
<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <Styles.Resources>

    <ControlTheme x:Key="TrestleBoard.PrimaryButton"
                  TargetType="Button"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground}" />
      <!-- The gold bar. A shape as well as a colour, so the emphasis survives a user who cannot
           tell the two fills apart (PLAN.md §6: colour is never the only signal). -->
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="5,0,0,0" />
      <!-- M76. The radius is a token for the same reason the colours are: a corner is a decision
           this application makes once. The primary keeps its accent fill and its gold bar exactly
           as M16 measured them — the only thing that changes here is the shape of the corner. -->
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Radius.Control}" />

      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Ornament.OnAccent}" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground}" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Focus}" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Accent.Foreground}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- UNAVAILABLE, and the reason this rule exists at all.

           Until M76 the primary treatment was worn only by dialog default buttons, none of which is
           ever disabled, so the state was unreachable and the rule was never written. M76 (e) put
           "Make the PDF..." on the toolbar and enrolled it in the availability pass, and
           newsletter.exportPdf is REFUSED with no newsletter open and refused again until the issue
           date is chosen — which is the state the application starts in and stays in for the whole
           of a freshly created issue. So the first thing a new user sees became the one control in
           the window falling through to Fluent's disabled treatment: accent fill, gold bar and
           readable label all replaced at once by the grey slab with the 2.61:1 label that M37
           measured on the action button below and called not a weak signal but an unreadable one.

           The refused primary therefore degrades to exactly the shape the action button's disabled
           state has — no border, no fill, the label in Chrome.Muted, which is a measured pair
           against Chrome.Background. That is deliberate and not merely convenient: a control that
           cannot be pressed must not keep the most emphatic treatment in the application, and it
           must still say what it is, because the reason it is unavailable is in its HelpText (M11)
           and that sentence is about a button the user has to be able to read the name of. -->
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Muted}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!--
      THE ACTION BUTTON (M37). What tells a user "you can press this".

      Measured from the shipped screenshots, an ENABLED toolbar button and a DISABLED one had the
      IDENTICAL fill — #BEBFC2 for both — and the only difference between them was the label's
      contrast: 11.42:1 against 2.61:1. So "grey slab" was simultaneously the look of a live button,
      the look of a dead one, and the look of the action panel's items, which are never disabled at
      all. The same appearance meant "press me" in one place and "you cannot" in another.

      The signal is now a BORDER, which is a shape and not only a colour (PLAN.md §6). Border
      present, label at full contrast: pressable. Border gone, fill gone, label dimmed: not.
      Removing the fill as well as the border matters — a disabled control that still has a slab
      under it is still competing for attention it does not deserve.

      Chrome.Border cannot serve as this border: it is 2.36:1 against the button fill, under §6's
      3:1 floor for a boundary that must be perceived. Chrome.Foreground is 9.68:1 and is what is
      used. Border.Thickness is a token so High Contrast can step it up, exactly as every other
      rule in the app does.

      M76: THE FILL IS NOW A TOKEN TOO. Until this milestone the enabled fill was whatever Fluent
      handed the button — the #BEBFC2 measured above — which is the one colour in the running
      application that was never in the palette and never in the contrast block. It is now
      Chrome.Surface, the raised plane, and Chrome.Background underneath it is the ground. That
      raises the label's own contrast as a side effect: Chrome.Foreground was 9.68:1 on the Fluent
      grey and is 17.79:1 on Surface in Light.

      THE TONAL STEP IS REINFORCEMENT, NOT THE BOUNDARY. Surface against Background is 1.15:1 and
      the palette declares it decorative on purpose. What says where this button ends is still the
      M37 border above — a shape — which is why that border is untouched here and why High Contrast,
      where Surface and Background are the same black, loses nothing: there the border steps to 2px
      and does the whole job alone. PLAN.md §6's "colour is never the only signal" is satisfied
      because colour is not ANY of the signal here, only comfort.
    -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TrestleBoard.ActionButton"
                  TargetType="Button"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Surface}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Border.Thickness}" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Radius.Control}" />

      <!-- Fluent's own :pointerover and :pressed rules target the template's ContentPresenter, so
           the border AND the fill have to be restated there or they vanish the instant the pointer
           arrives — the same trap the primary button above documents. Before M76 only the border
           needed restating, because there was no fill of ours to lose; now there is, and a fill set
           on the control alone would flash back to Fluent grey on hover.

           The hover fill is Chrome.BACKGROUND: the button sinks one step towards the ground rather
           than inventing a fourth grey. That choice is deliberate and not merely convenient — every
           foreground in this application has already been measured against Chrome.Background, so
           hovering a button cannot walk any pair under its floor, and a new hover colour would have
           owed the contrast block another seven lines to prove the same thing. -->
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Background}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Foreground}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Pressed keeps the sunk fill and swaps the border to Focus, so the press is a colour AND
           a state the border already announces. -->
      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Background}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Focus}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Unavailable: no border, no fill, and a label that can still be READ. Fluent's disabled
           foreground came out at 2.61:1 on the slab, which is not a weak signal but an unreadable
           one — an elderly user could not make out what the button they cannot press even says,
           and the reason it is unavailable is written in its HelpText (M11).

           M76 DELIBERATELY LEFT THIS ALONE. Giving the disabled state the new Surface fill would
           have undone the milestone above: a dead button with a raised plane under it is a slab
           again, and "no border, no fill" is precisely how this application says you cannot press
           this. The Transparent below is not an oversight to tidy up into a token. -->
      <Style Selector="^:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Chrome.Muted}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

    <!--
      M40. A button that REMOVES something, reviewed as "styled identically to neutral buttons".
      Based on the action treatment rather than replacing it, so it keeps the disabled rules that
      milestone settled and differs only where it should: a heavier outline, in the warning colour,
      with the label in the same colour.
    -->
    <ControlTheme x:Key="TrestleBoard.DestructiveButton"
                  TargetType="Button"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource TrestleBoard.ActionButton}">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Warning}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Focus.Thickness}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Warning}" />

      <!-- Restated on the template part for the reason the action theme documents: Fluent's own
           pointerover and pressed rules target the ContentPresenter, and would drop the outline the
           moment the pointer arrived - on the one button where that outline is the warning. -->
      <Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Warning}" />
        <Setter Property="TextElement.Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Warning}" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="^:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.Warning}" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>

  </Styles.Resources>

  <!--
    Opted into by class, not by a bare element selector, for the reason at the top of this file: a
    ScrollBar's repeat buttons and a ComboBox's drop-down toggle ARE Buttons, and would take this
    border with them. `Tokens.Action()` is the one way to ask for it, and ActionSurfaceTests walks
    every window asserting that every button the APP made has either this or the primary treatment
    — so a new button cannot quietly ship looking disabled.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button.action">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.ActionButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!-- M40. Two classes, most-specific wins: a destructive button is an action button first. -->
  <Style Selector="Button.action.destructive">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.DestructiveButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    DEFAULT-BUTTON EMPHASIS, STANDARDISED. Thirteen dialogs set IsDefault and every one of them then
    looked exactly like the button beside it, which is most of what "every button is the same grey
    slab" meant. A property-qualified selector reaches all thirteen at once and cannot miss the
    fourteenth; doing it by hand at each call site could, and did.

    M42. This block sits AFTER the two above, and the order is the whole point. Styles later in the
    collection win, so while it stood before them every button that was BOTH default and marked with
    Tokens.Action() came out looking like an ordinary one — the officers-sync dialog's "Update the
    table" and the import wizard's "Next" among them. The way onward in a dialog is the way onward
    whether or not somebody also asked for the action affordance.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button[IsDefault=True]">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{DynamicResource TrestleBoard.PrimaryButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    M45. Tooltips are new in this milestone, and PLAN.md §6's 16pt floor applies to them like
    everything else: a hint that explains a button, in text smaller than the button, would be the
    one part of the window this audience cannot read. They also wrap, because the catalog's
    descriptions are sentences rather than labels.
  -->
  <Style Selector="ToolTip">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16" />
    <Setter Property="MaxWidth" Value="420" />
  </Style>

  <Style Selector="ToolTip /template/ ContentPresenter">
    <Setter Property="TextBlock.TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    THE WINDOW ICON. There was no Icon= anywhere in src/ and no AvaloniaResource item in any .csproj:
    trestleboard.png reached the Windows installer, the AppImage and the .icns, and never the running
    application, which showed the platform's generic placeholder on all thirteen windows.

    This is the one bare element selector in the file, and it is safe for the reason the others are
    not: a Window is never nested inside another control's template, so there is no ComboBox toggle
    or ScrollBar repeat button that will quietly inherit it. It also sets an icon, not a metric.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Window">
    <Setter Property="Icon" Value="avares://TrestleBoard.App/Theme/trestleboard.png" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    M76 (c). THE CHROME'S TYPEFACE. Until now the document was set in bundled faces and the window
    around it was set in whatever font the operating system volunteered — Segoe UI here, Cantarell
    there, SF there — in an application whose central guarantee is that it looks the same on all
    three. Source Sans 3 is already bundled and already licensed (assets-src/fonts/fonts.json), it
    is humanist with open apertures, and it holds up at the 16pt floor PLAN.md §6 sets for the
    eyes this program exists for.

    The .ttf files reach here as AvaloniaResource LINKS out of assets-src/fonts — see the long note
    in TrestleBoard.App.csproj for why the Layout project's embedded copies cannot serve, and why
    no second .ttf was added to the repository to make this work.

    TOPLEVEL, NOT WINDOW. FontFamily inherits down the visual tree, so a Window setter would reach
    everything drawn inside the window — but a menu, a combo drop-down or a tooltip is hosted in a
    PopupRoot, which is a TopLevel of its own and would have kept the platform font. Selecting
    TopLevel catches both. It is safe on the same grounds the Window selector above is: a TopLevel
    is never nested inside another control's template, so nothing inherits this by accident.

    THIS IS NOT A SIZE. Every FontSize in the application is untouched; §6's 16pt floor and the
    18-20pt dialog sizes are set where they always were.
  -->
  <!--
    THE FALLBACK IS NOT DEFENSIVE PADDING, IT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A PLAIN FONT AND A CRASH.
    Avalonia resolves a FontFamily at RENDER time, and a family it cannot produce throws
    "Could not create glyphTypeface" out of the render pass — which is not a paragraph in the wrong
    typeface, it is a window that does not draw. That is exactly what happened the first time this
    setter shipped: the headless suite's font manager (UseHeadlessDrawing) cannot load an embedded
    family, and the throw landed inside a queued layout pass during dispatcher teardown, poisoning
    the session for every test after it.

    Naming $Default second means the worst case is the platform's own font, which is precisely what
    this application looked like before M76 — a graceful loss of a nicety, never a lost window.
  -->
  <Style Selector="TopLevel">
    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="avares://TrestleBoard.App/Theme/Fonts#Source Sans 3, $Default" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
